		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=17886#p17886"><![CDATA[
Hi all,<br><br>I've not been on the boards for a while, as I've been working on a different project, but thought I'd share with you what I've been using Bullet for, now it's gone public!<br><br>I work at <a href="http://framestore.com/" class="postlink">Framestore</a>, and spent several months last year developing a tool called "fBounce", which allows artists to run Bullet rigid body simulations through Maya. The tool has been used on a few productions now, but the only one released so far is Sherlock Holmes, which won the VES award for Outstanding Supporting Visual Effects in a Feature Motion Picture. There's an article on the <a href="http://www.fxguide.com/article597.html" class="postlink">fxguide website</a> about the effects, which mentions fBounce twice!<br><br>So, thanks to everyone on here who helped me out when I got stuck - I reckon that award must be a little bit yours too <img class="smilies" src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/images/smilies/icon_wink.gif" width="15" height="15" alt=";)" title="Wink"><br><br>Kate<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=3048">kate</a> — Tue Mar 16, 2010 2:48 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
